          "plan": {
            "A": "Build a low-risk sequential candidate. Retain accepted output/tie behavior while removing pervasive graph/vector copies, map/queue allocations, repeated tree-edge scans, and repeated color rescans; use contiguous/read-only graph data, tree adjacency, visitation stamps, and incremental color frequencies. Preserve generic correctness, including disconnected or isolated graphs, and provide an independently measured candidate.",
            "B": "Build an independent exact solver that searches simple paths directly rather than enumerating spanning trees. Use component preprocessing, DFS backtracking with visited vertices and color counts, an incumbent witness, and only mathematically safe reachable-color upper bounds; exhaustively fall back when no all-color witness is found. Heuristic ordering may improve discovery but must never determine correctness or rely on benchmark values.",
            "C": "Build a race-free four-core exact-search candidate. Split components, start vertices, or sufficiently coarse DFS prefixes into independent local searches; publish a monotonic incumbent and cancel only after reaching a proven global upper bound. Include a sequential fallback for small work, since thread startup may exceed benefit at the measured scale; preserve valid output and deterministic/accepted witness handling.",
            "G": "Independently validate and repeatedly measure A, B, and C against the unchanged harness. Treat candidates as alternatives first; test a combined version only when compatible, because gains are not additive. Select the fastest clearly valid result on ARM64, accounting for the 5.65 ms baseline noise floor, and retain the baseline/best single candidate if integration regresses.",
            "analysis": "The valid baseline averages 0.0056499 s (5.803, 5.598, 5.548 ms) on four ARM64 cores. The source has costly O(N²) storage and copies, map-based temporary traversal, repeated path/color scans, and constrained spanning-tree enumeration. Maximum distinct-color simple path requires an exact search in general, so no heuristic-only or expected-output shortcut is acceptable. All variants must preserve parsing/output requirements and handle arbitrary valid graphs and color repetitions; parallel overhead is a material risk at this runtime."
