            "A": "Prioritize the likely bottleneck: exact-output serialization. Produce a serial candidate that preserves the current sort and permutation traversal but replaces per-character iostream insertion and per-permutation `std::endl` flushing with a bounded buffered byte writer. Retain the precise `[ `, character-plus-space, `]\\n` layout, the six-character whitespace-removal rule, and empty-input behavior; never retain the entire factorial-sized output in memory.",
            "B": "Independently investigate a low-overhead serial permutation/formatting path. Compare the existing generic traversal with a specialized in-place string/line representation or an equivalent standard permutation primitive, while preserving the baseline’s character ordering, duplicate handling, lexicographic order, and byte-for-byte output. Focus on eliminating repeated formatting/callback work without relying on input-specific counts or precomputed answers; provide a separately measurable candidate rather than assuming it combines beneficially with A.",
            "C": "Assess whether four ARM64 cores can help despite the inherently ordered stdout stream. Explore only bounded-memory ordered decomposition of lexicographic permutation ranges/prefixes, with a single ordered emitter and exact handling of repeated characters. Output serialization is likely dominant, so reject parallel designs whose synchronization, buffering, or ordered merge loses to the best serial candidate; do not use unbounded whole-output buffering or alter the fixed build/target setup.",
